      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We are asked to find the magnitude, or the distance from the origin, of a complex number $a + bi$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers. In this case, we have $\\frac12 - \\frac38i$. To do this, we use the formula $|a + bi| = \\sqrt{(a^2 + b^2)}$.\n\nHere, $a$ and $b$ are the real and imaginary parts of the given complex number. So,\n$$\\left|\\frac12 - \\frac38i\\right| = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac12\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\frac38\\right)^2}$$\nSetting the second exponent to $2$, we obtain:\n$$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{9}{32}}$$\nConverting fractions $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{9}{32}$ to equivalent fractions with a common denominator, we have the following equation:\n$$\\sqrt{ \\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{9}{32} } $$\nWe simplify the fractions with the common denominator:\n$$\\sqrt{ \\frac {2}{8} + \\frac {9}{32} }$$\n$$\\sqrt{ \\frac {8}{32} + \\frac {9}{32} } $$\n$$\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{32}}$$\n\nSo, combining the fractions according to their denominators and simplifying with a common denominator to think about the final answer, $\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{32}}$ equals to\n$\\boxed{\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{32}}}$",
